HST Office: HST Sheets reads LAMBDA and LET parameter lists typed with either separator, and keeps typed
optional LAMBDA parameters ([b]) optional through .xlsx and .ods.

- ScCompiler::GetPossibleParaCount() counted only the configured separator, while ';' is accepted as a
  parameter separator in every grammar but OOXML. With English settings (',' separator) a typed
  =LAMBDA(x;x*2)(21) or =LET(f;LAMBDA(x;x+1);f(2)) took its first name for a body reference and gave
  #VALUE!/#NAME?. The count now takes ';' where ParseOpCode() does, skips string literals and stops at the
  binding's closing parenthesis (it also counted the separators of a following call, LAMBDA(p;q;p+q)(1;2)).
  The same code is in LibreOffice master.
- A typed [b] stayed as separate bracket tokens and only the compiled code knew it was optional: .xlsx got
  [_xlpm.b], which the .xlsx reader takes for an external reference, and .ods (ODFF has no bracket symbol)
  got _xlpm.b, a required parameter. The tokenizer now drops the brackets and marks the name token as
  optional (as for _xlop.b); the writer gives _xlop.b in OOXML and ODFF and [b] in the UI grammars.
  ODFF reads _xlop. (hst-sheets-lambda-functions.patch).

Test: CppunitTest_sc_excelcompat_functions_test (its document uses ',' as separator; ';' formulas, the
_xlop.b text in .xlsx, a call after a LAMBDA, ';' inside a string), tests/runtime/check-sheets-excel-functions.py.
